{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfuQjXAdi9VYFoBMLzkRS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. The rule is disabled, because the tool creates rules that way, so it won't fire until you enable it. I didn't enable it because you only asked for it to exist.\n\n**Data:** The \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" view is web access logs with a `timestamp` field. Its main fields are `response` (the HTTP status code, stored as a string), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `machine.os`, `host`, `url`, `agent` and `referer`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-ede2034d Web logs overview\" has four panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Requests by response code\n- Average bytes by extension\n\nI didn't open it in Kibana to check how the panels render. Its URL is `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-ede2034d-web-logs-overview-9d359e194ff1`.\n\n**Rule:** \"EFF-ede2034d 5xx spike\" (id `66a4d573-ee33-487f-9440-504c233a61bb`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and counts documents whose `response.keyword` matches `5[0-9][0-9]` over the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is above 10. It has no notification action attached, so even once enabled it will only record alerts inside Kibana."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":655,"cache_read_input_tokens":20254,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":655},"output_tokens":481,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0536cbf5-c97a-5467-80bd-e50c5d30a2ce","uuid":"f3ed4f4d-196a-4e18-8a2c-e3d4c354da50","timestamp":"2026-10-10T23:17:36.226Z","request_id":"req_011CfuQjWvkbwhL6RXT9MBJg"}
